{
  "title": "How many, what time",
  "title_target": "Quot? Quota hora est?",
  "goal": "Count to a thousand, use ordinal numbers, give your age and the time the Roman way, and read Roman numerals on buildings and in books.",
  "bekal": [
    "count to a hundred and write the numbers in Roman numerals",
    "answer *quot annos habes?* correctly",
    "tell the time with *hora* and an ordinal number"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Colloquium — In taberna Publii",
      "intro": "At Publius's grain shop. Quintus never pays the first price.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Publius:",
          "text": "Salve, Quinte! Quid hodie emis?",
          "id": "Hello, Quintus! What are you buying today?",
          "literal": "be-well, Quintus-call! what today buy-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Frumentum emo. Quot modii in taberna sunt?",
          "id": "I'm buying grain. How many measures are in the shop?",
          "literal": "grain-obj buy-I. how-many measure-pl in shop-in are-they?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Publius:",
          "text": "Viginti modii sunt. Et boni sunt!",
          "id": "There are twenty measures. And they're good!",
          "literal": "twenty measure-pl are-they. and good-pl are-they!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Bene. Quot denarios postulas?",
          "id": "Good. How many denarii are you asking?",
          "literal": "well. how-many denarius-pl-obj demand-you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Publius:",
          "text": "Decem denarios. Pretium bonum est!",
          "id": "Ten denarii. That's a good price!",
          "literal": "ten denarius-pl-obj. price good-neut is-it!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Decem? Nimium est! Octo do.",
          "id": "Ten? That's too much! I'll give eight.",
          "literal": "ten? too-much is-it! eight give-I."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Publius:",
          "text": "Octo? Novem, et frumentum tuum est.",
          "id": "Eight? Nine, and the grain is yours.",
          "literal": "eight? nine, and grain your-neut is-it."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Novem. Bene! Ecce pecunia.",
          "id": "Nine. All right! Here's the money.",
          "literal": "nine. well! look money."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Marcus:",
          "text": "Pater, quota hora est?",
          "id": "Dad, what time is it?",
          "literal": "father-call, which-numbered hour is-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Hora quinta est. Cena hora nona est.",
          "id": "It's the fifth hour. Dinner is at the ninth hour.",
          "literal": "hour fifth is-it. dinner hour-with ninth-with is-it."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Marcus:",
          "text": "Quattuor horae! Esurio iam nunc!",
          "id": "Four hours! I'm hungry already!",
          "literal": "four hour-pl! be-hungry-I already now!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Quintus:",
          "text": "Patientia, fili! Quot annos habes? Tredecim — iam vir es.",
          "id": "Patience, son! How old are you? Thirteen — you're a man already.",
          "literal": "patience, son-call! how-many year-pl-obj have-you? thirteen — already man are-you."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Quot annos habes? — age is something you \"have\".** Literally \"how many years do you have?\", and the answer is *… annos habeo*. Spanish, French and Italian inherited this from Latin (*tengo trece años*), whereas English says \"I am thirteen\". A second way also appears often on gravestones: *annos tredecim natus* — \"born thirteen years\".",
        "**Hora quinta isn't five in the morning.** The Romans split the time between sunrise and sunset into twelve equal hours. Because daylight changes with the seasons, an *hora* in June was longer than one in December. *Hora prima* began at sunrise, so *hora quinta* fell around ten or eleven in the morning.",
        "**Quota hora est? — an ordinal, not a counting number.** Romans didn't ask the time with counting numbers, but with ordinals: \"which-numbered hour?\". The answer is an ordinal too: *hora prima, secunda, tertia*. The habit survives in church bells and in how we number centuries.",
        "**Denarius, modius — units best left as they are.** A *denarius* was a silver coin roughly equal to a laborer's daily wage; a *modius* was a grain measure of about eight to nine liters. *Denarius* lives on in the *dinar*, and in the *d* that stood for \"penny\" in old British money."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Counting to a thousand",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Good news for beginners: apart from the first three numbers and the hundreds from two hundred up, Latin counting numbers **never change form**. You only need to learn how they sound."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
