        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ordinals behave like ordinary adjectives and change outfits to match their noun: *hora prima*, *liber primus*, *bellum primum*. Roman names like Quintus and Sextus really do come from ordinals — the fifth child and the sixth child."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Latin numbers you already use",
          "items": [
            "*unus* → **uni**versity, **uni**form, **uni**que. *duo* → **du**et, **du**al. *tres* → **tri**o, **tri**ple.",
            "*quattuor* → **quar**tet, **quar**ter. *quinque* → **quin**tet. *sex* → **sex**tet. *centum* → **cent**ury, per**cent**.",
            "*primus* → **prim**e, **prim**ary. *secundus* → **second**. *ultimus* → **ultim**atum.",
            "*mille* → **mil**limeter, **mill**ennium. Note that *mille* means a thousand, while *milia* means thousands."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Roman hours and Roman numerals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "To tell the time in Latin, use an ordinal with the word *hora*. The form used is the \"with/in\" form, because time counts as a circumstance."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Latin",
            "Meaning",
            "Roughly covers"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "hora prima",
              "the first hour",
              "6:00–7:00 a.m. — starts at sunrise"
            ],
            [
              "hora tertia",
              "the third hour",
              "8:00–9:00 a.m."
            ],
            [
              "hora sexta",
              "the sixth hour",
              "11:00 a.m.–12:00 — ends at midday"
            ],
            [
              "hora nona",
              "the ninth hour",
              "2:00–3:00 p.m."
            ],
            [
              "hora duodecima",
              "the twelfth hour",
              "5:00–6:00 p.m. — until sunset"
            ],
            [
              "media nocte",
              "midnight",
              "12:00 a.m."
            ],
            [
              "mane",
              "in the morning",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "vespere",
              "in the evening",
              "—"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "*Hora sexta* is midday, and from it Spanish got *siesta* — the nap at the sixth hour. *Hora nona* gave English the word *noon*, even though its meaning shifted three hours during the Middle Ages."
        },
